From dcba2c6ef3043d7f0a60e78e5ba264183a34c4a1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ngo Quoc Dat <datlechin@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 09:56:56 +0700
Subject: [PATCH 3/7] vrend: limit what contexts can make vrend hold

A context's command streams make vrend allocate a GL context for each
sub-context, objects, a transform feedback object for each new set of
streamout targets, and shader text, bounded only by the host's memory. A
VMM can bound resources and their bytes itself, but not these without
parsing every stream as the decoder does.

virgl_renderer_set_limits() takes limits on GL contexts, objects and
shader bytes, for each context and for all contexts together, and on the
text and tokens one shader declares. The shader bytes are the TGSI text
of the shaders vrend keeps and of those still arriving, and the tokens a
shader is parsed into while it is parsed. vrend counts each as it
allocates and frees it. The limits on one shader also bound the GLSL vrend
writes from it, which is not counted. A command that would go past a
limit is not run and fails its context with the new
VIRGL_ERROR_CTX_LIMIT_EXCEEDED, as a malformed command does; a context
that would pass the total of GL contexts is not created. The renderer's
own context is not counted. Without a call, or with a limit of 0, nothing
changes.

The tests cover each limit, a replaced handle, objects that go with their
sub-context or context, and other contexts that go on.

Signed-off-by: Ngo Quoc Dat <datlechin@gmail.com>
